.TH "JWT\-VERIFY" "1" "" "jwt\-verify User Manual" "LibJWT C Library"
.SH NAME
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] \- Verify a JSON Web Token
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] \f[B][options]\f[R] \f[I]token\f[R] \&...
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] \f[B][options]\f[R] \- < \f[I]token\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] Decodes and (optionally) verifies the signature of
a JSON Web Token.
.PP
By default, the token(s) will be decoded and verified.
If there is a signature block on the JWT, then you must give a JWK key
with the \f[B]\-k\f[R] option, or a JWK Set with the \f[B]\-r\f[R]
option.
Verifying a signature with \f[B]\-k\f[R] requires specifying the
algorithm, so it must either be in the key file (as the \f[B]alg\f[R]
attribute), or passed on the command line with the \f[B]\-a\f[R]
argument.
.PP
An unsigned token (\f[B]\(lqalg\(rq:\(lqnone\(rq\f[R]) is rejected
unless \f[B]\-a none\f[R] is given with neither \f[B]\-k\f[R] nor
\f[B]\-r\f[R] (RFC 8725 §3.2); such a token is then only decoded, since
there is no signature to check.
.PP
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] will not assume the algorithm from the JWT itself
(for security reasons), however, the algorithm in the JWT must match
what you provide to \f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R].
.PP
Tokens may be passed on the command line, after any options, separated
by spaces, or passed via \f[B]stdin\f[R], one per line.
To use \f[B]stdin\f[R], you must pass \f[B]\-\f[R] as the last and only
argument after any options.
.PP
Each line of \f[B]stdin\f[R] is one token, and the last line does not
need a newline.
An empty line is a token that fails.
A line of 1048576 bytes (1 MiB) or more, not counting its newline, or
one that has a NUL byte in it, is not verified, not even in part, and
counts as one token that failed.
.PP
A token on the command line is held to the library\(cqs default maximum
token size of 1048576 bytes (1 MiB): a longer one fails with \(lqToken
too large\(rq and is not parsed.
.PP
When using the \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R] option, \f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] will
print the JSON \f[I]HEADER\f[R] and \f[I]PAYLOAD\f[R] to
\f[B]stdout\f[R].
.PP
If used in conjunction with \f[B]\-\-print\f[R], the JSON will be piped
to the command\(cqs \f[B]stdin\f[R].
It will be called twice: once for \f[I]HEAD\f[R] and once for
\f[I]PAYLOAD\f[R].
.PP
One use is to pass it through \f[B]jq \-C\f[R] for indenting and
colorization.
Another would be to use an external program to validate the
\f[I]PAYLOAD\f[R] contents.
A non\-0 exit status from the program will cause verification to fail,
and so will the program being ended by a signal.
.PP
The command need not read all of its input: if it stops reading,
\f[B]jwt\-verify\f[R] is not ended (no matter how large the JSON is),
and the program\(cqs exit status, or a signal that ends it, still
decides whether the token passes.
.SS Options
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Show common options and quit.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-list\f[R]
List all supported algorithms that can be passed to the \f[B]\-a\f[R]
option and quit.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Show the contents of the \f[I]HEADER\f[R] and \f[I]PAYLOAD\f[R] of the
JWT in addition to verifying the token.
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Do not output anything except for hard errors.
The result is the exit status: see \f[B]EXIT STATUS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R] \f[I]ALG\f[R], \f[B]\-\-algorithm\f[R]=\f[I]ALG\f[R]
Specify the algorithm to be used when verifying the signature block of
any tokens passed to the program.
See \f[B]\-l\f[R] for values of \f[I]ALG\f[R].
Not accepted with \f[B]\-\-keyring\f[R], where a key\(cqs own
\f[B]alg\f[R], if it has one, pins the algorithm.
With neither \f[B]\-k\f[R] nor \f[B]\-r\f[R], \f[B]\-a none\f[R] accepts
an unsigned (\f[B]\(lqalg\(rq:\(lqnone\(rq\f[R]) token, which is then
only decoded; without it, such a token is rejected.
.TP
\f[B]\-k\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], \f[B]\-\-key\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path to a file containing a key in JSON Web Key format.
If your keys are in PEM or DER (or some other common format that
\f[I]OpenSSL\f[R] understands), then you can convert it to a JWK with
the \f[B]key2jwk(1)\f[R] tool.
.TP
\f[B]\-r\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keyring\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path to a file containing a JWK Set (JWKS) of candidate keys, used to
verify Compact tokens and the (multi\-signature) JWS JSON Serialization
(RFC 7515 §7.2).
A signature naming a \f[B]kid\f[R] is matched to that key; a keyless
signature is tried against every compatible key.
A key whose \f[B]use\f[R] is not \f[B]sig\f[R], or whose
\f[B]key_ops\f[R] names neither \f[B]verify\f[R] nor \f[B]sign\f[R], is
not a candidate.
Mutually exclusive with \f[B]\-\-key\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-P\f[R] \f[I]POLICY\f[R], \f[B]\-\-policy\f[R]=\f[I]POLICY\f[R]
Multi\-signature acceptance policy when a keyring is used: \f[B]any\f[R]
(the default \(em accept if at least one signature verifies) or
\f[B]all\f[R] (every signature in the token must verify).
.TP
\f[B]\-p\f[R] \f[I]CMD\f[R], \f[B]\-\-print\f[R]=\f[I]CMD\f[R]
Pipe JSON of header and payload to \f[I]CMD\f[R] through its
\f[B]stdin\f[R].
Requires \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]; without it the command would never run,
so it is a usage error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Every token was verified: its signature checked with \f[B]\-k\f[R] or
\f[B]\-r\f[R], or, with neither, the token is unsigned and \f[B]\-a
none\f[R] was given, so it is only decoded.
With neither and no \f[B]\-a none\f[R], every token fails.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
A token failed, there was no token to verify (nothing to read on
\f[B]stdin\f[R]), or the program could not do its work: a bad option, a
key or keyring that could not be loaded, a read error.
.PP
The exit status is not the number of tokens that failed.
It is 1 for one failure and for any number of them.
.PP
\f[B]\-\-help\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-list\f[R] verify nothing; given alone
they exit 0, but given with anything else (a token, a key, another
option) they are a usage error (exit 1).
An argument that starts with \f[B]\-\f[R] is read as an option unless it
comes after \f[B]\-\-\f[R], and a \f[B]\-\f[R] on its own selects
\f[B]stdin\f[R] even after \f[B]\-\-\f[R].
To check a string that comes from somewhere you do not trust, write it
to \f[B]stdin\f[R] as a single line, or reject it first if it starts
with \f[B]\-\f[R], which a token never does.
